  "question": {
    "text": "A monoatomic ideal gas undergoes a process in which the ratio of $P$ to $V$ at any instant is constant and equals to 1 . What is the molar heat capacity of the gas?",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "7rt41",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{4 R}{2}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "5aJyY",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{3 R}{2}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "B6rhS",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{5 R}{2}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "zqoCl",
      "text": "\n0",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "The molar heat capacity for any process is given by a following expression\n$C=C_V+\\frac{R}{1-\\gamma}$ when $p V^\\gamma=$ constant and $C_p / C_V=\\gamma$\nHere, $\\frac{p}{V}=1$ i.e., $p V^{-1}=$ constant\n$$\n\\therefore \\quad C=\\frac{3}{2} R+\\frac{R}{1-(-1)}=\\frac{3}{2} R+\\frac{R}{2}=\\frac{4}{2} R\n$$",
    "image": null
  },
